From 886686818b163f0e747d687cf5068a2cfed9ac8b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <claude@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 12:13:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Migrate Print to the async command client; wire in tipper
 parameters

Print now uses IMaticaCommandClient throughout instead of MachineComm.
CommandManagement - the last remaining consumer of the synchronous
MachineCommands path. Step order is unchanged: ready check, LoadCard,
ReadMAG, Backend Call #1, Emboss, local batch write, EjectCard, Backend
Call #2.

Failure handling changed shape, not behavior. The async client throws
typed MachineExceptions instead of returning a reply code:
- The ready check and LoadCard let a thrown exception propagate straight
  to GlobalExceptionMiddleware - there's nothing to eject yet if either
  fails, matching the previous behavior exactly.
- ReadMAG and Backend Call #1 both eject the card before reporting
  failure, exactly as before, including the original's own quirk:
  if the eject attempt itself also fails, the eject failure is reported
  instead of the original error, not alongside it. Extracted into
  EjectThenFailAsync since both sites needed the identical sequence.
- Emboss and the final EjectCard are still caught locally rather than
  left to propagate, since the flow must continue to the local batch
  write and Backend Call #2 regardless of the physical outcome - this
  mirrors the previous `bool printSucceeded = !MachineComm.ValuateReply
  (Reply)` pattern exactly, just sourced from a caught exception instead
  of a reply code.

Two new error categories (BackendRejected/BackendUnavailable, 409/502)
distinguish "the Inventory API rejected this business-wise" from
"the physical machine rejected this" - MachineRejected covers the
latter. Both map to the standard ApiResponse<T> contract like every
other error in this service now does; Print's success response is
PrintResult (Printed/ResultConfirmedWithBackend/ProductItemId/
IdempotencyKey) instead of an anonymous object, and the "printed but
could not confirm with the backend" case still returns HTTP 207 with
Success: true, since the card itself is fine.

Tipper parameters (approved decision, "read from printer"): added as
four optional int fields on PrintReqDto (TipperTemperature/Pressure/
Consumption/Time), defaulting to 0 exactly like the Inventory API's
MaticaPrinterConfiguration fields added alongside this patch. Angular
supplies them the same way it already supplies Font/Cpi/OffsetX/OffsetY
- fetched from the printer's config, not looked up by this service.
EmbossAsync forwards them straight through to EmbossLineClass's existing
constructor parameters, which already accepted all four; nothing in the
machine protocol changed, only where the values come from.

MaticaCommandClient gained LoadCardAsync, ReadMagAsync (returns the raw
track data - ReadMAG's whole purpose - via a new ParseData helper
alongside the existing EnsureAccepted, which only validates), and
EmbossAsync.

Now genuinely dead, left in place deliberately: MachineComm and its
supporting DI-injected fields (ConnectionInfo, MachineInfo, httpAction,
Data) on MachineController - Print was their last consumer. Same reason
as everything else deferred this far: MachineCommands.cs itself and its
DI registrations are the actual hardware-communication code that has not
been through the hardware validation this whole migration has been
gated on step by step. Removing the now-dead references without also
removing what they reference would just be half a cleanup; both wait for
the same validation pass together.

Companion Inventory API patch: adds TipperTemperature/Pressure/
Consumption/Time to MaticaPrinterConfiguration - required for Angular to
have real values to supply here rather than every printer permanently
sending 0.

No dotnet SDK or NuGet access in the authoring environment. Verified by
the symbol-table cross-check (one false positive confirmed and dismissed
- a regex over "record struct" misparsing "struct" as a type name;
confirmed by grep that MaticaCommandClient never references MachineError
directly), brace-balance across every touched file, and JSON/XML
validity on both resx files. Please build locally before merging, and
this is the step where hardware validation matters most - Print is the
one endpoint every card issuance actually depends on.
